freeCAD: 3D CAD with Motion Simulation  by  askoh.com
Assembly
	Notes
		(Text string: 'CAD: Copyright (C) 2000-2001, Aik-Siong Koh, All Rights Reserved.<n><n>A rectangular slab is hinged (revolute joint) to the ground and allowed to swing the the xy plane. A torsional spring is applied at the hinge to resists rotation of the slab. A second slab is hinged to the first at the other end. Another torsional spring resists relative rotation of the two slabs. Similarly, a third slab is added. The whole system is allowed to oscillate under gravity like a cantilever.<n><n>If the instructions below are too brief, refer to the Notes in projectile.asm and circular.asm.<n><n>To load the example for a quick look:<n>Click File/Open/Assembly/ to get a dialog. Enter *.asm for a list of assemblies. Select torsion.asm. <n><n>To create the assembly from scratch:<n>To create the three rectangular slabs:<n>Create an empty assembly and populate it with three slabs (Assembly1Part1, Assembly1Part2, Assembly1Part3) each of dimensions (1.0d, 0.2d, 1.0d). Set the density of each slab to (10.0d).<n><n>To mark the joint attachment points:<n>Create a marker (Assembly1Marker1) on the assembly at (0.0d, 0.0d, 0.0d).<n>On the first slab, create a marker (Assembly1Part1Marker1) at (-0.1d, 0.1d, 0.0d) and another (Assembly1Part1Marker2) at (1.1d, 0.1d, 0.0d) relative to the z-face. <n>Since the three slabs are identical, only one needs to be created from scratch and saved with markers in a *.prt file. Then that *.prt can be inserted into the assembly twice.<n><n>To create the revolute joints:<n>Connect (Assembly1Marker1) to (Assembly1Part1Marker1) with revolute joint (Assembly1Joint1).<n>Connect (Assembly1Part1Marker2) to (Assembly1Part2Marker1) with revolute joint (Assembly1Joint2).<n>Connect (Assembly1Part2Marker2) to (Assembly1Part3Marker1) with revolute joint (Assembly1Joint3).<n><n>To connect the slabs with torsional spring-damper combinations:<n>Connect (Assembly1Marker1) to (Assembly1Part1Marker1) with Dynamic/ForceTorque/General (Assembly1Torque1).<n>Click radio button ''Tz''. Click Example/SpringDamper/TorqueZ/Linear to get the equation<n>T0 + KT*(angleIJz(self) - A0) + CT*omeIJKi(self, I, 3)<n>where T0 = set torque, KT = stiffness, angleIJz() = angle about common z from I to J, A0 = set angle, CT = damping coeff., omeIJKi(self, K, i) = omega from I to J in Ki component, self = the ForceTorque containing the equation.<n>Subsititute T0 = 0, KT = (10*180/pi), A0 = 0, CT = 0 to get the formula<n>0 + (10*180/pi)*(angleIJz(self) - 0) + 0*omeIJKi(self, I, 3)<n>Click the radio button ''Tz'' again to accept the equation. The other equations are not needed and can be left empty. Click OK.<n>There is no harm leaving those zero terms or constant expressions in the equation for clarity. The symbolic parser simplifies all equations for internal use.<n>We do not use the ForceTorque/InLine because rIJ(self) would be zero and the torque direction would be undefined.<n>Connect (Assembly1Part1Marker2) to (Assembly1Part2Marker1) with Dynamic/ForceTorque/General (Assembly1Torque2).<n>Enter Tz<n>0 + (10*180/pi)*(angleIJz(self) - 0) + 0*omeIJKi(self, I, 3)<n>Connect (Assembly1Part2Marker2) to (Assembly1Part3Marker1) with Dynamic/ForceTorque/General (Assembly1Torque3).<n>Enter Tz<n>0 + (10*180/pi)*(angleIJz(self) - 0) + 0*omeIJKi(self, I, 3)<n><n>To set the initial conditions:<n>It is possible to set the initial positions and initial velocities of the cylinders precisely by RightClick/PositionPart/Direction/ and RightClick/Velocity/ over any part marker.<n><n>To set gravity:<n>Click Dynamic/Gravity/ for the gravity dialog. Enter (0.0d, -9.81d, 0.0d).<n><n>The assembly is now ready for simulation, animation and plotting.<n>Run a Quasi Static simulation to find the static equillibrium configuration of the system.<n>' runs: (Core.RunArray runs: #(475 63 14 5 12 1 2 37 89 11 4 36 1 38 189 36 417 30 291 63 1374 30 181 15 234) values: #(nil #underline #(#underline #bold) #underline #(#underline #bold) #underline nil #(#bold #large) nil #bold nil #(#bold #large) nil #bold nil #bold nil #bold nil #bold nil #bold nil #bold nil)))
